    "output": "Archlin\u2019s Phase 1 investment is focused on supply management for its specialty chemicals manufacturing business, with the primary business driver being improvement of poor inventory planning. The expected value of this phase should therefore be evaluated through the extent to which Archlin can improve planning effectiveness, strengthen inventory visibility and control, and reduce avoidable supply chain costs associated with suboptimal inventory decisions. The available source context identifies SAP S/$HANA as Archlin\u2019s ERP environment; any detailed target-state integration architecture, site-level deployment scope, baseline performance values, quantified savings, implementation dates, and named solution components are .\n\nArchlin\u2019s current pain point of poor inventory planning creates a direct need for measurable benefit realization around supply management. The business case for the investment should be anchored in benefits such as better alignment of supply with demand, improved inventory planning discipline, reduced inventory-related inefficiencies, and stronger operational decision-making. Specific quantified targets for lost sales reduction, forecast accuracy improvement, obsolete inventory reduction, inventory holding cost reduction, expedited freight reduction, gross margin improvement, planner productivity, overtime reduction, or one-time financial benefits are .\n\nFor Archlin, benefit realization should be governed by a defined baseline-and-target approach, even where exact values remain TBD. Baseline measurements should be established for the inventory planning and supply management indicators that Archlin chooses to manage, and the same metrics should be monitored after implementation to confirm realized value. Because the source documents do not provide specific current-state values or target thresholds, all baseline values, improvement targets, reporting cadence, business owners, and validation method are TBD.\n\nThe identified value drivers for Archlin are tied to supply chain planning outcomes rather than to a technical implementation alone. Poor inventory planning should be translated into business outcomes that Archlin can monitor, such as inventory availability, inventory quality, inventory carrying impact, planning workload, and avoidable supply chain cost. The source context does not provide Archlin-specific financial estimates for annual savings, conservative and realistic benefit ranges, premium freight reduction, planner efficiency, overtime reduction, or gross margin improvement; these values are therefore and should be confirmed through Archlin\u2019s business case and benefit tracking process.\n\nBenefit realization for Archlin should remain aligned to the Phase 1 supply management scope. This means that any value measurement should focus on the business processes and capabilities included in that phase, and should not assume additional deployment scope, broader functional coverage, or enterprise-wide benefits unless confirmed in Archlin source documents. The confirmed implementation partner context is that Bristlecone is acting as the system integrator for Archlin; the investment and business benefits described in this document relate to Archlin\u2019s supply management transformation.",
