  "5": {
    "status": "generated",
    "output": "Archlin operates in the specialty chemicals manufacturing industry and currently uses SAP S/4HANA as its ERP system. The current planning process is Excel-based, with no centralized planning platform across North America and Europe. Inventory levels are higher than target due to poor forecast visibility, and manual Excel processes cause delays in monthly planning cycles.\n\nArchlin\u2019s demand planners spend significant time consolidating forecasts from multiple regions. The current forecast accuracy is approximately 68%, which contributes to inventory planning challenges and reduced visibility for supply planning decisions. Supply planners have highlighted recurring issues with raw material shortages and requested exception-based alerts to improve responsiveness.\n\nArchlin\u2019s supply planning capabilities are expected to support constrained planning, material shortage visibility, capacity planning, alerts, and scenario analysis.\n\n| Requirement Area | Requirement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Supply Planning | Generate constrained supply plans. |\n| Supply Planning | Identify material shortages. |\n| Supply Planning | Support capacity planning. |\n| Supply Planning | Provide exception-based alerts. |\n| Supply Planning | Enable scenario analysis. |\n\nArchlin\u2019s inventory planning capabilities are expected to support safety stock policies, inventory target calculation, excess inventory monitoring, inventory turns tracking, and stock optimization across locations.\n\n| Requirement Area | Requirement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Inventory Planning | Define safety stock policies. |\n| Inventory Planning | Calculate inventory targets. |\n| Inventory Planning | Monitor excess inventory. |\n| Inventory Planning | Track inventory turns. |\n| Inventory Planning | Optimize stock across locations. |\n\nArchlin plans to implement Kinaxis RapidResponse for Demand Planning, Supply Planning, and Inventory Optimization. For Phase 1, the scope is supply management. The proposed supply and inventory planning capability will provide Archlin with a centralized planning approach to improve supply visibility, inventory target management, and response to raw material shortages.\n\nThe solution will support generation of constrained supply plans, identification of material shortages, and capacity planning. Exception-based alerts will be used to help supply planners focus on recurring raw material shortage issues. Scenario analysis will enable Archlin to evaluate planning alternatives before committing to supply decisions.\n\nFor inventory planning, the solution will support definition of safety stock policies, calculation of inventory targets, monitoring of excess inventory, tracking of inventory turns, and optimization of stock across locations. These capabilities directly address Archlin\u2019s challenge of inventory levels being higher than target due to poor forecast visibility.\n\n<IMAGE id=\"b4392383fdc1\"/>\n\nArchlin\u2019s supply and inventory planning process will require integration with key master and transactional data from SAP S/4HANA and related planning data sources. The required interfaces identified in the source documents are listed below.\n\nRequired Interfaces\n\n| Interface |\n| --- |\n| Product Master |\n| Customer Master |\n| Sales Orders |\n| Inventory Balances |\n| Purchase Orders |\n| Production Orders |\n\nThe data refresh frequency identified for Archlin is every 4 hours.\n\n| Data Element | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| Data Refresh | Every 4 hours |\n\nArchlin\u2019s supply and inventory planning process will be integrated with demand planning outputs to improve forecast visibility and support supply planning decisions. Business users emphasized the need for collaborative forecasting and visibility across regions. Demand planners requested automated forecast generation with the ability to manually override forecasts when market conditions change.\n\nSupply planning will use demand and supply inputs to generate constrained supply plans, identify material shortages, support capacity planning, and provide exception-based alerts. Inventory planning will use supply and inventory inputs to define safety stock policies, calculate inventory targets, monitor excess inventory, track inventory turns, and optimize stock across locations.\n\nFor Archlin, the implementation of supply and inventory planning should focus on enabling a single source of planning data and reducing reliance on manual Excel-based processes. The implementation should account for the required interfaces, the every 4 hours data refresh frequency, and the Phase 1 scope of supply management.\n\nKey implementation considerations include:\n\nEstablishing integration for Product Master, Customer Master, Sales Orders, Inventory Balances, Purchase Orders, and Production Orders.\n\nSupporting constrained supply planning to improve visibility of supply feasibility.\n\nEnabling material shortage identification to address recurring raw material shortage issues.\n\nConfiguring exception-based alerts for supply planning.\n\nSupporting scenario analysis for supply planning decisions.\n\nDefining safety stock policies and inventory target calculations.\n\nMonitoring excess inventory and tracking inventory turns.\n\nSupporting stock optimization across locations.\n\nArchlin expects measurable improvements in forecast accuracy and inventory reduction within six months of go-live. The success criteria identified for the planning implementation include forecast accuracy improvement, inventory reduction, planning cycle reduction, improved planner productivity, and a single source of planning data.\n\n| Success Criterion | Target / Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| Forecast Accuracy | > 85% |\n| Inventory Reduction | > 15% |\n| Planning Cycle Reduction | > 30% |\n| Planner Productivity | Improved Planner Productivity |\n| Planning Data | Single Source of Planning Data |\n\nFor supply and inventory planning, these outcomes are expected to support Archlin\u2019s objective to improve inventory planning, reduce excess inventory, increase planning visibility, and enable more responsive management of raw material shortages.",
